WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.101 --> 00:00:02.469
Right now, the Senate racing

00:00:02.469 --> 00:00:04.237
to pass a crucial debt ceiling bill.

00:00:04.237 --> 00:00:06.039
That bipartisan deal cleared the House

00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:06.873
last night.

00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:08.108
That, of course, after months

00:00:08.108 --> 00:00:09.542
of infighting and often bitter

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:11.077
negotiation is the final tally, though.

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:13.947
Look at this. 314 to 117.

00:00:13.947 --> 00:00:17.350
149 Republicans and 165 Democrats

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:18.985
voted yes in favor.

00:00:18.985 --> 00:00:20.286
71 Republicans

00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:22.489
and 46 Democrats voted against.

00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:24.791
Senators must follow suit by Monday,

00:00:24.791 --> 00:00:26.159
or the Treasury Department says

00:00:26.159 --> 00:00:27.827
America could start defaulting

00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:29.462
on some of its bills.

00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:30.063
The Senate majority

00:00:30.063 --> 00:00:30.730
leader, Chuck Schumer

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:31.598
then telling the chamber

00:00:31.598 --> 00:00:34.467
this morning, buckle up.

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:35.702
Time is a luxury.

00:00:35.702 --> 00:00:37.937
The Senate does not have any needless

00:00:37.937 --> 00:00:39.806
delay or any last minute hold ups.

00:00:39.806 --> 00:00:41.508
Would be an unnecessary

00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:44.110
and even dangerous risk.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:46.346
And any change to this bill

00:00:46.346 --> 00:00:47.781
that forces us to send it

00:00:47.781 --> 00:00:49.249
back to the House

00:00:49.249 --> 00:00:50.984
would be entirely unacceptable.

00:00:50.984 --> 00:00:53.720
It would almost guarantee default.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.221
Here in studio to share their reporting

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:56.022
and their insights.

00:00:56.022 --> 00:00:57.757
CNN's Melanie Sloan,

00:00:57.757 --> 00:00:59.426
already part of The Washington Post.

00:00:59.426 --> 00:01:01.795
And Margaret Talev of Axios.

00:01:01.795 --> 00:01:03.496
So walk us through this.

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:04.364
Explain to America

00:01:04.364 --> 00:01:06.066
how Washington and the Senate works.

00:01:06.066 --> 00:01:06.800
Senator Schumer,

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.301
Senator McConnell have an agreement.

00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:09.235
Some senators will get

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:10.837
to propose amendments

00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:12.439
as long as they can kill them,

00:01:12.439 --> 00:01:13.773
long as they make sure they lose

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:15.241
so that they don't change the House bill

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:16.242
and have to send it back.

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:16.876
So in the Senate,

00:01:16.876 --> 00:01:18.978
any single member can slow things down,

00:01:19.012 --> 00:01:19.412
hold it up.

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:20.313
They need the cooperation

00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:21.848
of every single senator

00:01:21.848 --> 00:01:23.316
in order to move quickly on this.

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:25.118
So what the leadership is doing

00:01:25.118 --> 00:01:26.319
is going to offer them

00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:28.021
amendment votes in exchange

00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:29.389
for their cooperation.

00:01:29.389 --> 00:01:30.824
These amendment votes will fail.

00:01:30.824 --> 00:01:31.724
Everyone knows that,

00:01:31.724 --> 00:01:32.592
because if it were to pass,

00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:34.027
that would completely upset things.

00:01:34.027 --> 00:01:35.061
This is a very delicately

00:01:35.061 --> 00:01:35.929
negotiated deal.

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:37.230
It would have to go back to the House.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:38.064
But they're in the process

00:01:38.064 --> 00:01:39.165
of working that out

00:01:39.165 --> 00:01:41.067
It is just a matter of when,

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:43.069
not if they pass this in the Senate.

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:43.603
We're hearing

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:44.571
it might be sometime

00:01:44.571 --> 00:01:45.538
today, might be tomorrow,

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:46.306
but it's going to get done

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:47.807
hopefully before the weekend.

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:49.175
So they'll have the amendments to do.

00:01:49.175 --> 00:01:50.343
You write an interesting post,

00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:50.910
The Washington Post

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.312
in the piece in The Washington Post

00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:53.346
saying we have Democratic president,

00:01:53.346 --> 00:01:54.781
we have a Republican speaker,

00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:55.982
and yet they negotiate a deal

00:01:55.982 --> 00:01:57.484
and they're both in some ways, winners.

00:01:57.484 --> 00:01:58.351
Now, there are some Nixon

00:01:58.351 --> 00:01:59.319
both of them from this process.

00:01:59.319 --> 00:02:00.520
But I want to read in the piece

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:01.988
the comments reflecting an emboldened

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:03.156
mood on the part of both men,

00:02:03.156 --> 00:02:04.457
an octogenarian Democrat

00:02:04.457 --> 00:02:04.924
who is faced

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:05.859
questions about his age

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:06.726
and ability to shepherd

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:08.094
a vibrant, diverse party

00:02:08.094 --> 00:02:09.362
and a California Republican

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:09.963
who has been viewed

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:11.264
as a weak leader, beholden

00:02:11.264 --> 00:02:12.132
to his conference's

00:02:12.132 --> 00:02:13.333
conservative firebrands.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:15.335
It is true, right?

00:02:15.335 --> 00:02:15.602
Yes.

00:02:15.602 --> 00:02:16.970
They both have some questions

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:17.537
going forward.

00:02:17.537 --> 00:02:18.138
From this,

00:02:18.138 --> 00:02:21.174
but they can both rightfully say, I win.

00:02:21.541 --> 00:02:22.408
And they are.

00:02:22.408 --> 00:02:22.742
There are

00:02:22.742 --> 00:02:23.443
I've already started

00:02:23.443 --> 00:02:25.345
to sort of brag about this deal, say

00:02:25.345 --> 00:02:26.146
this is how Washington

00:02:26.146 --> 00:02:27.080
is supposed to work.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:28.014
You have to remember that

00:02:28.014 --> 00:02:28.948
President Biden

00:02:28.948 --> 00:02:30.517
has faced questions about his age,

00:02:30.517 --> 00:02:31.251
about his ability,

00:02:31.251 --> 00:02:32.085
about whether or not

00:02:32.085 --> 00:02:33.353
he should be running for reelection

00:02:33.353 --> 00:02:34.154
in this

00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:34.921
divided government

00:02:34.921 --> 00:02:36.289
period as a test of whether or not he's

00:02:36.289 --> 00:02:38.024
going to be able to carry on

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:39.025
through the next election.

00:02:39.025 --> 00:02:40.026
And for McCarthy,

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:41.494
he faced challenges over

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:42.562
whether or not he should be speaker.

00:02:42.562 --> 00:02:44.097
He's been speaker for a few months.

00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:46.099
And there's this sort of sword

00:02:46.099 --> 00:02:47.300
hanging over his head over

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:48.001
whether or not

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:49.536
he will be able to remain in his post

00:02:49.536 --> 00:02:50.336
because of

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:51.538
some of the things that he's doing

00:02:51.538 --> 00:02:52.672
that are not supported

00:02:52.672 --> 00:02:53.907
by folks on the far right,

00:02:53.907 --> 00:02:55.909
but because he was able to get this deal

00:02:55.909 --> 00:02:57.210
and there aren't any real

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:58.811
major calls yet for his ouster.

00:02:58.811 --> 00:03:00.547
He can claim victory over this.

00:03:00.547 --> 00:03:02.882
And so there will be some out there.

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:04.050
And it's your job

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.552
to rain on their parade.

00:03:05.552 --> 00:03:06.686
Who would say, okay, well,

00:03:06.686 --> 00:03:08.087
you just did this, right?

00:03:08.087 --> 00:03:09.489
You just did this. Liberals are unhappy.

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:10.256
Conservatives aren't happy,

00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:11.024
but you just came together

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:12.892
in the broad middle of Washington,

00:03:12.892 --> 00:03:14.360
just did something very important.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:15.929
So why can't we have a conversation

00:03:15.929 --> 00:03:16.663
about immigration?

00:03:16.663 --> 00:03:17.830
Why can't we have a conversation

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:18.932
about whether they're big health care

00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:20.767
questions, entitlements,

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:22.035
child tax credits?

00:03:22.035 --> 00:03:23.303
Not going to happen, right?

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:24.671
Because the global economy

00:03:24.671 --> 00:03:25.071
is not going

00:03:25.071 --> 00:03:27.240
to collapse in tens of thousands,

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.241
if not more of Americans

00:03:28.241 --> 00:03:29.209
are going to lose their jobs.

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:29.576
The U.S.

00:03:29.576 --> 00:03:31.077
says they're going to be permanently

00:03:31.077 --> 00:03:32.645
knocked off of its

00:03:32.645 --> 00:03:33.446
leadership post

00:03:33.446 --> 00:03:33.780
in terms

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.148
of how the rest of the world

00:03:35.148 --> 00:03:37.116
of using the currency

00:03:37.116 --> 00:03:38.384
as the primary currency is

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:40.019
is not going to be shaken

00:03:40.019 --> 00:03:41.754
by any of those other things.

00:03:41.754 --> 00:03:44.357
The default has a singular capacity

00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:44.991
to have

00:03:44.991 --> 00:03:46.960
national and international shockwaves

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:48.561
that will be both immediate

00:03:48.561 --> 00:03:50.597
and permanent to some degree.

00:03:50.597 --> 00:03:51.030
And Kevin

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.532
McCarthy knows that, and so does

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:54.300
most of the Republican caucus.

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:55.902
I do think that

00:03:55.902 --> 00:03:57.270
this is a win for both men,

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:58.471
but in different ways.

00:03:58.471 --> 00:03:59.105
For Biden,

00:03:59.105 --> 00:04:00.139
there was nowhere to go

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:01.941
but down for the president.

00:04:01.941 --> 00:04:03.209
If it had been a default,

00:04:03.209 --> 00:04:04.143
it would have been a disaster.

00:04:04.143 --> 00:04:05.211
For the country, but also,

00:04:05.211 --> 00:04:06.379
you know, for him.

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:09.482
But it doesn't boost his his ratings

00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:10.350
or his standing

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:12.185
that he managed to stave it off.

00:04:12.185 --> 00:04:13.886
I don't think for McCarthy

00:04:13.886 --> 00:04:16.489
this is really a window to to reset

00:04:16.923 --> 00:04:18.691
both his own caucus and Americans

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:20.326
impression of his leadership skills

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:21.361
and of what kind of a speaker

00:04:21.361 --> 00:04:22.128
he's going to be.

00:04:22.128 --> 00:04:23.696
I don't know how far he can take that

00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:24.597
with a razor

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:25.265
thin majority

00:04:25.265 --> 00:04:26.099
that just at least

00:04:26.099 --> 00:04:28.434
temporarily got one smaller.

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:30.603
But but it is a moment

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.339
for for the for his caucus

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:35.842
and for the political establishment

00:04:35.842 --> 00:04:37.977
and Americans to say

00:04:37.977 --> 00:04:38.911
there might be more here

00:04:38.911 --> 00:04:40.046
than we thought there was.

00:04:40.046 --> 00:04:42.548
There are some still to McCarthy's right

00:04:42.548 --> 00:04:43.283
who are

00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:44.550
he had to fight to get their votes

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:45.251
to be speaker what?

00:04:45.251 --> 00:04:47.220
15 ballots back in January.

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:47.687
There are some

00:04:47.687 --> 00:04:48.988
who are unhappy and say

00:04:48.988 --> 00:04:51.024
they will consider using their power

00:04:51.024 --> 00:04:51.691
as part of the deal

00:04:51.691 --> 00:04:53.059
with any one member can move to vote

00:04:53.059 --> 00:04:54.527
but vacate the chair.

00:04:54.527 --> 00:04:55.728
Listen to one of them Ken Buck

00:04:58.031 --> 00:04:59.832
the discussion about the motion to vacate

00:04:59.832 --> 00:05:00.366
is going to happen

00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:01.467
in the next week or two.

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:02.602
What would your reaction be

00:05:02.602 --> 00:05:04.070
if Democrats were to step in

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:05.505
and save McCarthy?

00:05:05.505 --> 00:05:07.173
Oh, motion to vacate Vincente Gonzales

00:05:07.173 --> 00:05:08.107
just told us he's open.

00:05:08.107 --> 00:05:11.010
It's like a date with a unique

00:05:11.944 --> 00:05:12.745
collaborator.

00:05:12.745 --> 00:05:13.279
Yeah,

00:05:14.414 --> 00:05:16.249
he does not have much power

00:05:16.249 --> 00:05:18.284
when when he relies on Democrats

00:05:18.518 --> 00:05:20.253
to keep him as speaker.

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:22.388
Is that real

00:05:22.388 --> 00:05:24.123
meaning there will be a real challenge

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:25.558
to McCarthy's leadership.

00:05:25.558 --> 00:05:26.626
I assume he would survive it,

00:05:26.626 --> 00:05:27.727
but it would still be a dent

00:05:27.727 --> 00:05:29.095
or is that just venting?

00:05:29.095 --> 00:05:29.996
We'll have to see.

00:05:29.996 --> 00:05:31.464
I mean, they have the power,

00:05:31.464 --> 00:05:32.365
a single member to be able

00:05:32.365 --> 00:05:33.333
to at least call the vote.

00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:34.600
It would take five Republicans

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.035
to actually succeed,

00:05:36.035 --> 00:05:36.903
assuming all Democrats

00:05:36.903 --> 00:05:37.737
vote against McCarthy.

00:05:37.737 --> 00:05:39.238
In that scenario,

00:05:39.238 --> 00:05:41.040
Kevin McCarthy is not taking it lightly.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:42.475
He has to take that seriously.

00:05:42.475 --> 00:05:43.810
But he's also not sweating it.

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:45.445
And it's because, John, as you mentioned,

00:05:45.445 --> 00:05:47.080
he has beaten back

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:48.114
the conservative revolt

00:05:48.114 --> 00:05:48.948
before it took him

00:05:48.948 --> 00:05:50.350
15 ugly rounds of voting

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:51.751
but he eventually got there.

00:05:51.751 --> 00:05:53.386
I think what we're really determining

00:05:53.386 --> 00:05:55.588
is what members here, when they go back

00:05:55.588 --> 00:05:56.522
to their districts, right.

00:05:56.522 --> 00:05:58.291
If they are hearing conservatives

00:05:58.291 --> 00:05:59.459
who are furious back

00:05:59.459 --> 00:06:00.860
home, they might get fired up

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:01.861
and come back

00:06:01.861 --> 00:06:03.429
and want to proceed with this.

00:06:03.429 --> 00:06:05.798
But the fact that they are not right now

00:06:05.798 --> 00:06:07.333
committing to follow through with it,

00:06:07.333 --> 00:06:08.868
I think is probably a good sign.

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:09.802
That's an interesting point.

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:10.937
You make about when they go home

00:06:10.937 --> 00:06:12.605
because the leading Republican candidates

00:06:12.605 --> 00:06:14.340
for president now, Trump was late to it.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:15.341
He waited and waited and waited.

00:06:15.341 --> 00:06:17.577
But on I radio, I say he said no

00:06:17.577 --> 00:06:18.578
is a safe place

00:06:18.578 --> 00:06:20.246
for if you're appealing to the Republican

00:06:20.246 --> 00:06:21.781
base is to be no.

00:06:21.781 --> 00:06:22.715
So we'll see if some of that

00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:23.282
when they go home,

00:06:23.282 --> 00:06:24.350
if they come back to Washington,

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:25.184
if that happens.

00:06:25.184 --> 00:06:26.719
Manu, what are you hearing about

00:06:26.719 --> 00:06:28.154
how swiftly

00:06:28.154 --> 00:06:29.589
this bill could wind up

00:06:29.589 --> 00:06:30.990
on President Biden's desk?

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:33.426
Well, this is actually the subject

00:06:33.426 --> 00:06:34.994
of intense negotiations

00:06:34.994 --> 00:06:36.329
that are happening right now

00:06:36.329 --> 00:06:37.296
on the Senate floor.

00:06:37.296 --> 00:06:38.598
I just on my way over here

00:06:38.598 --> 00:06:40.166
to talk to the number two Republican,

00:06:40.166 --> 00:06:40.933
John Thune,

00:06:40.933 --> 00:06:42.735
who is deep in these negotiations

00:06:42.735 --> 00:06:44.237
to try to get this bill passed.

00:06:44.237 --> 00:06:45.071
As soon as tonight.

00:06:45.071 --> 00:06:46.305
He is having discussions

00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:47.740
with some members on the Senate floor,

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:48.341
including Senator

00:06:48.341 --> 00:06:49.175
Lindsey Graham

00:06:49.175 --> 00:06:49.942
and others

00:06:49.942 --> 00:06:52.111
who are demanding certain certain things.

00:06:52.111 --> 00:06:53.780
One thing that Lindsey Graham wants

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:55.348
is he wants some sort of insurance

00:06:55.348 --> 00:06:56.549
from Democratic leaders

00:06:56.549 --> 00:06:57.650
and Republican leaders

00:06:57.650 --> 00:06:59.051
that they will move on to

00:06:59.051 --> 00:07:01.087
Ukraine a funding package

00:07:01.087 --> 00:07:02.522
sometime later this year.

00:07:02.522 --> 00:07:03.856
There's also some concerns

00:07:03.856 --> 00:07:05.024
about defense funding

00:07:05.024 --> 00:07:05.992
and the level of defense

00:07:05.992 --> 00:07:07.560
funding in this plan, a deal

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.262
that was cut between Mick McCarthy

00:07:09.262 --> 00:07:09.896
and the White House

00:07:09.896 --> 00:07:11.097
to essentially level out

00:07:11.097 --> 00:07:11.998
funding, provide

00:07:11.998 --> 00:07:12.398
the funding

00:07:12.398 --> 00:07:14.567
that was under the Biden

00:07:14.567 --> 00:07:15.168
proposal,

00:07:15.168 --> 00:07:16.502
the Biden administration's defense

00:07:16.502 --> 00:07:17.437
spending proposal.

00:07:17.437 --> 00:07:18.538
That is not far enough

00:07:18.538 --> 00:07:19.238
for a number of these

00:07:19.238 --> 00:07:20.773
hawks on the Republican side of the aisle

00:07:20.773 --> 00:07:21.541
who believe

00:07:21.541 --> 00:07:22.975
that it needs to be much further

00:07:22.975 --> 00:07:23.943
believe it has not kept up

00:07:23.943 --> 00:07:25.044
with the pace of inflation.

00:07:25.044 --> 00:07:26.712
And all these concerns are significant

00:07:26.712 --> 00:07:27.613
because one

00:07:27.613 --> 00:07:28.881
senators, single senators

00:07:28.881 --> 00:07:31.884
can delay matters until past a Monday,

00:07:31.984 --> 00:07:32.852
the fall deadline.

00:07:32.852 --> 00:07:34.487
They need to get all to agree

00:07:34.487 --> 00:07:36.122
to actually schedule the vote

00:07:36.122 --> 00:07:36.689
and the moment

00:07:36.689 --> 00:07:38.124
they don't have that agreement.

00:07:38.124 --> 00:07:38.991
Now, this all comes

00:07:38.991 --> 00:07:40.560
as senators are raising concerns.

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:41.961
Lindsey Graham told me today

00:07:41.961 --> 00:07:43.930
that this deal was a bad one that was cut

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:46.432
and others are also uncertain about

00:07:46.432 --> 00:07:48.501
whether they're going to back this plan

00:07:48.501 --> 00:07:49.235
to is

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:51.871
This is really dumb.

00:07:51.871 --> 00:07:54.207
The people who negotiated this,

00:07:54.207 --> 00:07:55.975
I wouldn't let them buy me a car.

00:07:55.975 --> 00:07:57.310
The speaker said the Democrats

00:07:57.310 --> 00:07:59.645
didn't get any wins here.

00:07:59.946 --> 00:08:01.414
Well, I'll tell you why.

00:08:01.414 --> 00:08:01.948
A lot of them

00:08:01.948 --> 00:08:02.648
believe they did

00:08:02.648 --> 00:08:04.150
because more Democrats voted

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:05.718
for it than Republicans.

00:08:05.718 --> 00:08:06.652
What does that say to you?

00:08:06.652 --> 00:08:09.155
I know in a divided government is tough

00:08:09.155 --> 00:08:10.723
but don't tell me that

00:08:10.723 --> 00:08:12.558
this is the best we could do.

00:08:12.558 --> 00:08:13.659
The party of Ronald Reagan

00:08:13.659 --> 00:08:14.527
is in jeopardy.

00:08:14.527 --> 00:08:15.895
This is really dumb.

00:08:15.895 --> 00:08:18.631
This is the wrong way to address it in

00:08:18.631 --> 00:08:19.532
just the wrong way.

00:08:19.532 --> 00:08:20.066
It

00:08:20.833 --> 00:08:24.070
empowers the folks on the far right.

00:08:25.471 --> 00:08:26.272
And quite frankly,

00:08:26.272 --> 00:08:26.973
I don't think they have

00:08:26.973 --> 00:08:28.174
the best interests of the country.

00:08:28.174 --> 00:08:28.841
In mind.

00:08:29.308 --> 00:08:30.142
And

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:33.212
I don't think I haven't talked to anybody

00:08:33.212 --> 00:08:35.414
that's enamored with this deal.

00:08:36.482 --> 00:08:38.417
But it's really more a question of today.

00:08:38.417 --> 00:08:40.019
So there are concerns on the left.

00:08:40.019 --> 00:08:41.387
I believe the White House gave

00:08:41.387 --> 00:08:41.854
too much

00:08:41.854 --> 00:08:43.556
to the speaker, concerns on the right

00:08:43.556 --> 00:08:44.690
that the speaker gave too much

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:45.191
the White House.

00:08:45.191 --> 00:08:46.726
But there's also no doubt about that.

00:08:46.726 --> 00:08:48.027
There is expectation

00:08:48.027 --> 00:08:49.262
that this will pass

00:08:49.262 --> 00:08:50.596
eventually, maybe tonight,

00:08:50.596 --> 00:08:51.464
maybe tomorrow.

00:08:51.464 --> 00:08:53.132
Senators are begrudgingly voting

00:08:53.132 --> 00:08:54.066
for a compromise

00:08:54.066 --> 00:08:54.700
they don't love,

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:55.368
but they realize

00:08:55.368 --> 00:08:57.870
this is the only way to avoid a default

00:08:58.571 --> 00:09:00.706
They have bipartisan disdain

00:09:00.706 --> 00:09:01.607
for this bill.

00:09:01.607 --> 00:09:03.943
It seems that's a sign of a compromise.

00:09:03.943 --> 00:09:05.511
Monterrosa, live on Capitol Hill,

00:09:05.511 --> 00:09:06.512
thanks so much.

00:09:06.512 --> 00:09:07.079
And Brianna,

00:09:07.079 --> 00:09:07.680
this is more

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:09.282
than just about raising the debt ceiling.

00:09:09.282 --> 00:09:11.384
This has implications far and wide.

00:09:11.751 --> 00:09:12.451
Yeah, certainly

00:09:12.451 --> 00:09:13.586
if passed into law,

00:09:13.586 --> 00:09:14.720
the bipartisan debt

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:15.688
deal would prevent

00:09:15.688 --> 00:09:16.722
the Biden administration

00:09:16.722 --> 00:09:18.791
from extending the federal student

00:09:18.791 --> 00:09:19.458
loan payment

00:09:19.458 --> 00:09:21.027
pause that began in 20, 20

00:09:21.027 --> 00:09:23.162
during the Trump administration.

00:09:23.162 --> 00:09:25.765
The bill reads that the pause shall cease

00:09:25.765 --> 00:09:28.634
to be effective. 60 days after June 30th.

00:09:28.634 --> 00:09:30.036
And that would mean

00:09:30.036 --> 00:09:32.305
that roughly 44 million

00:09:32.305 --> 00:09:33.539
people will have to resume

00:09:33.539 --> 00:09:35.174
making payments on their loans.

00:09:35.174 --> 00:09:36.375
And there is some concern

00:09:36.375 --> 00:09:38.244
if this process is going to go smoothly.

00:09:38.244 --> 00:09:39.011
As you can imagine,

00:09:39.011 --> 00:09:39.946
there are so many people

00:09:39.946 --> 00:09:40.880
who may be confused

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:42.648
about how much they owe,

00:09:42.648 --> 00:09:44.784
when to pay, how they're going to pay.

00:09:44.784 --> 00:09:47.587
Especially when you consider this

00:09:47.587 --> 00:09:49.555
that 2 million borrowers

00:09:49.555 --> 00:09:51.424
will have a different servicer

00:09:51.424 --> 00:09:52.558
handling their loans

00:09:52.558 --> 00:09:53.626
since the last time

00:09:53.626 --> 00:09:55.127
that they made a payment.

00:09:55.127 --> 00:09:56.295
In the meantime,

00:09:56.295 --> 00:09:58.197
all eyes are going to be on

00:09:58.197 --> 00:09:58.965
the Supreme Court.

00:09:58.965 --> 00:10:00.333
Justices could rule

00:10:00.333 --> 00:10:00.933
as early

00:10:00.933 --> 00:10:03.402
as this month on a separate student loan

00:10:03.603 --> 00:10:05.271
forgiveness program.

00:10:05.271 --> 00:10:07.106
And under the Biden

00:10:07.106 --> 00:10:08.341
administration, proposal,

00:10:08.341 --> 00:10:09.609
individual borrowers

00:10:09.609 --> 00:10:12.812
who made less than $125,000 in either

00:10:12.812 --> 00:10:14.880
20, 20 or 20, 21,

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:16.349
and also married couples

00:10:16.349 --> 00:10:17.817
or heads of households

00:10:17.817 --> 00:10:20.620
who made less than $250,000 per year,

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:25.291
they would qualify for up to $10,000

00:10:25.291 --> 00:10:26.258
of their federal student

00:10:26.258 --> 00:10:27.693
loan debt being forgiven

00:10:27.693 --> 00:10:30.229
and if a borrower got a Pell

00:10:30.229 --> 00:10:31.330
Grant in college,

00:10:31.330 --> 00:10:32.598
if they got a Pell Grant in college,

00:10:32.598 --> 00:10:34.400
that individual is eligible

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:37.770
for up to 20 grand in debt forgiveness.

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:39.939
If the Supreme Court

00:10:39.939 --> 00:10:41.207
allows this program

00:10:41.207 --> 00:10:43.242
to take effect, it's possible

00:10:43.242 --> 00:10:44.844
the government moves quickly

00:10:44.844 --> 00:10:45.678
to forgive the debts

00:10:45.678 --> 00:10:47.613
of 16 million borrowers

00:10:47.613 --> 00:10:49.982
who are already approved for relief.

00:10:49.982 --> 00:10:51.517
If the justices strike down

00:10:51.517 --> 00:10:52.151
Biden, the student

00:10:52.151 --> 00:10:53.719
loan forgiveness program,

00:10:53.719 --> 00:10:54.520
the administration

00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:56.422
could make some modifications.

00:10:56.422 --> 00:10:57.857
They could try again

00:10:57.857 --> 00:10:58.624
But that, of course,

00:10:58.624 --> 00:10:59.525
could take months, Jim.

